       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="what-is-military-time-sec" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="What is Military Time">
    <h2>2. What is Military Time?</h2>
    <p>
      Military Time is a method of tracking time based on a continuous 24-hour scale. The day starts at midnight (represented as **0000**) and runs through the 24 hours of the day, ending at 2359. Unlike standard civil timekeeping, it does not use AM or PM markers to differentiate morning from afternoon.
    </p>
    <p>
      The term "Military Time" is predominantly used in the United States and Canada, where the civilian population relies almost exclusively on the 12-hour clock. In most other parts of the world, this system is referred to as the **24-hour clock** or standard international time.
    </p>
    <p>
      The system's origin dates back to ancient civilizations. The Egyptians were among the first to divide the day into 24 parts using sundials and stellar observations. Specifically, they divided the day into 10 hours of daylight, 2 hours of twilight, and 12 hours of night. However, the modern implementation of a synchronized 24-hour clock began during the late 19th and early 20th centuries as navies and armies required a standard, unified method to synchronize operations across large distances.
    </p>
    <p>
      In historical naval operations, ship bells were rung at half-hour intervals to signal watches, but this localized system was insufficient for coordinate warfare involving land forces, railways, and telegraphs. The adoption of the 24-hour clock standard by the British Royal Navy in 1915 and the United States Army during World War I marked the birth of "Military Time" as we know it today.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 3: WHY MILITARY TIME EXISTS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="why-military-time-exists" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Why Military Time Exists">
    <h2>3. Why Military Time Exists</h2>
    <p>
      The primary reason for adopting the 24-hour clock is the elimination of ambiguity. In standard timekeeping, the letters 'AM' and 'PM' can easily be misread, written down incorrectly, or omitted entirely. In high-stakes environments, such as medical treatment, tactical warfare, aviation scheduling, or network logging, a timezone mistake can have catastrophic consequences.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Preventing Medical Errors</strong>: Hospitals operate on 24-hour schedules. If a nurse logs that a patient received medication at 8:00, it is unclear whether that occurred at 8:00 AM or 8:00 PM. By using **0800** and **2000**, the timeline is unambiguous. This ensures patient safety during shift handovers when staff are fatigued.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Aviation Coordination</strong>: Aircraft traverse several time zones in a single flight. Coordinating arrival slots and runway schedules in standard time would result in timezone offsets clashing. Standardizing flight manifests to the 24-hour UTC clock solves this issue. Every international airport, commercial carrier, and air traffic control tower references a single, non-shifting 24-hour baseline.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Military Operations</strong>: Coordination of ground, air, and naval forces requires absolute synchronization. A command issued to strike at "0600" is completely distinct from a command at "1800." In combat scenarios where communication channels may be static-ridden or compromised, a four-digit numeric command leaves zero room for misinterpretation.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Custom Inline SVG: 12-hour vs 24-hour infographic (Infographic 2) -->
    <div style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
