  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 6: 12-HOUR VS 24-HOUR CLOCK COMPARISON
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="12-vs-24-hour-crossover" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="12-Hour vs 24-Hour Comparison">
    <h2>6. 12-Hour vs 24-Hour Clock Comparison</h2>
    <p>
      While standard time is simple for daily social schedules, the 24-hour clock possesses structural advantages that make it standard in critical networks:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>No AM/PM Errors</strong>: Standard time requires parsing secondary string markers (AM/PM) which can cause script parser errors or manual entry mistakes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Clear Chronological Order</strong>: 24-hour time values naturally sort in chronological order (e.g., 0800 comes before 1300), which is extremely useful for database developers compiling timestamps.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Uniform Formatting</strong>: Standard time can be formatted with varying layouts (e.g., "7 PM," "07:00 PM," "7:00 p.m."). Military time uses a fixed four-digit syntax (0700 or 1900).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table aria-label="Comparison of 12-hour and 24-hour formats">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Feature</th>
            <th>12-Hour Standard Clock</th>
            <th>24-Hour Military Clock</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Day Division</strong>:</td>
            <td>Two blocks of 12 hours (AM &amp; PM)</td>
            <td>One continuous block of 24 hours</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Clarity</strong>:</td>
            <td>Subject to AM/PM confusion</td>
            <td>100% unambiguous</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>String Length</strong>:</td>
            <td>Variable (e.g., "8 PM" vs "08:00 PM")</td>
            <td>Always four digits (e.g., "2000")</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Common Sectors</strong>:</td>
            <td>Social, retail, everyday conversation</td>
            <td>Aviation, military, hospitals, databases</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 7: CONVERTING STANDARD TO MILITARY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="convert-standard-to-military" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Converting Standard to Military">
    <h2>7. Converting Standard Time to Military Time</h2>
    <p>
      Converting standard civil time (12-hour AM/PM) to military time (24-hour) requires checking the phase of the day (morning vs. afternoon) and applying consistent mathematical rules:
    </p>
    
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Rule 1: AM Hours (1:00 AM to 11:59 AM)</h3>
      <p>
        For standard AM hours, the numbers remain identical. Keep the hour and minutes exactly the same, add a leading zero to the hour if it is a single digit (from 1 to 9), and remove the colon and space:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Standard Time: 8:30 AM &rarr; Keep hours/mins (8 and 30) &rarr; Add leading zero &rarr; <strong>0830</strong></li>
        <li>Standard Time: 11:15 AM &rarr; Keep hours/mins (11 and 15) &rarr; No leading zero needed &rarr; <strong>1115</strong></li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Rule 2: PM Hours (1:00 PM to 11:59 PM)</h3>
      <p>
        For standard PM hours, you must add 12 to the hour value. Keep the minutes identical, add the numbers, and remove the colon:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Standard Time: 1:00 PM &rarr; Add 12 to hour (1 + 12 = 13) &rarr; Keep minutes (00) &rarr; <strong>1300</strong></li>
        <li>Standard Time: 6:45 PM &rarr; Add 12 to hour (6 + 12 = 18) &rarr; Keep minutes (45) &rarr; <strong>1845</strong></li>
        <li>Standard Time: 10:15 PM &rarr; Add 12 to hour (10 + 12 = 22) &rarr; Keep minutes (15) &rarr; <strong>2215</strong></li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Rule 3: Noon Hour (12:00 PM to 12:59 PM)</h3>
      <p>
        Noon represents the midpoint of the day. Do not add 12 to the hour. Keep the hour as 12, keep the minutes identical, and remove the colon:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Standard Time: 12:00 PM &rarr; Keep hour (12) &rarr; Keep minutes (00) &rarr; <strong>1200</strong></li>
        <li>Standard Time: 12:30 PM &rarr; Keep hour (12) &rarr; Keep minutes (30) &rarr; <strong>1230</strong></li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Rule 4: Midnight Hours (12:00 AM to 12:59 AM)</h3>
      <p>
        Midnight represents the beginning of a new calendar date. Change the hour '12' to '00', keep the minutes identical, and remove the colon:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Standard Time: 12:00 AM &rarr; Change 12 to 00 &rarr; Keep minutes (00) &rarr; <strong>0000</strong></li>
        <li>Standard Time: 12:15 AM &rarr; Change 12 to 00 &rarr; Keep minutes (15) &rarr; <strong>0015</strong></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 8: CONVERTING MILITARY TO STANDARD
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="convert-military-to-standard" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Converting Military to Standard">
    <h2>8. Converting Military Time to Standard Time</h2>
    <p>
      To convert a military clock timestamp back to the standard 12-hour format, use these steps:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>If the military hour is between 0000 and 0059</strong>: The time is AM. Change the first two digits to 12, insert a colon, and append 'AM' (e.g., 0015 becomes 12:15 AM).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>If the military hour is between 0100 and 1159</strong>: The time is AM. Remove the leading zero if present, insert a colon, and append 'AM' (e.g., 0830 becomes 8:30 AM).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>If the military hour is between 1200 and 1259</strong>: The time is PM. Keep the hour as 12, insert a colon, and append 'PM' (e.g., 1245 becomes 12:45 PM).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>If the military hour is 1300 or greater</strong>: The time is PM. Subtract 12 from the hour, insert a colon, and append 'PM' (e.g., 2115 becomes 9:15 PM; 21 - 12 = 9).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 9: INDUSTRY EXAMPLES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="industry-examples" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Industry-Specific Examples">
    <h2>9. Industry-Specific Examples</h2>
    <p>
      Many civil and scientific industries require the use of the 24-hour clock to maintain coordination. In these sectors, standard time is rarely used because the risks associated with AM/PM confusion are too high:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Healthcare &amp; Hospital Charting</strong>: In hospitals, patient records, medication administration, surgical procedures, and lab reports are documented using the 24-hour clock. For example, if a patient is scheduled to receive a critical dose of insulin at 0800 and another at 2000, there is no chance that a nurse will administer both doses in the morning. Shift handovers are also coordinated using 24-hour shifts, preventing confusion during transition periods when incoming and outgoing staff review logs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Aviation and Air Traffic Control</strong>: Commercial pilots and air traffic controllers coordinate flight plans using Coordinated Universal Time (UTC), which is formatted in 24-hour military time. Regardless of the local timezone of the departure or arrival airport, flight logs, gate allocations, and weather briefings reference a single global 24-hour timeline. This prevents errors when planes cross multiple time zones in rapid succession.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Emergency Services</strong>: Police forces, fire departments, and ambulance services log incident reports using the 24-hour clock. This creates a clear chronological timeline for criminal investigations, emergency responses, and insurance reports. For instance, an incident logged at 1545 establishes an unambiguous sequence of events that cannot be confused with a similar event at 0345.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Maritime Coordination</strong>: Ocean-going vessels coordinate watch cycles, navigational logs, and weather warnings using the 24-hour clock. Because ships cross meridians of longitude, maintaining a standard 24-hour clock (referenced to GMT/UTC) ensures that coordinates and logs remain synchronized with global maritime safety systems.
      </li>
    </ul>

