       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="global-adoption" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Global Adoption">
    <h2>10. Military Time Around the World</h2>
    <p>
      While standard 12-hour time is popular in North America, the 24-hour clock is the standard civil format in most countries in Europe, Asia, Latin America, and Africa. In these regions, public transit timetables, digital signs, store operating hours, and television schedules are displayed in the 24-hour format:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Europe</strong>: Train timetables, flight arrivals, and television program listings are uniformly written in the 24-hour format (e.g., a train departing at 17:30). In spoken language, residents of European countries often translate these values back into 12-hour expressions in casual conversation (e.g., saying "half past five" instead of "seventeen thirty").
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Latin America</strong>: Official government documents, business schedules, and school timetables utilize the 24-hour clock. In spoken Spanish or Portuguese, standard time markers (e.g., "de la tarde" or "de la noche") are common, but written communication is almost exclusively 24-hour.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>United Kingdom &amp; Australia</strong>: The UK and Australia use a hybrid model. Standard 12-hour time is common in casual spoken English, while the 24-hour clock is the default format for digital schedules, train timetables, and official business.
      </li>
    </ul>

