       SECTION 11: MILITARY TIME AND TIME ZONES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="timezones-zulu" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Time Zones and Zulu Time">
    <h2>11. Military Time &amp; Time Zones (Zulu Time)</h2>
    <p>
      In international military and aviation systems, operations are synchronized to Coordinated Universal Time (UTC), which is referred to as **Zulu Time** (Z). The zero meridian timezone is designated by the letter Z.
    </p>
    <p>
      The military divides the globe into 25 distinct timezone bands, each designated by a letter of the alphabet. This is known as the **Military Time Zone System**:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Zulu Time (Z)</strong>: Centered on the Prime Meridian (0° longitude). Used as the global baseline for all military operations.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Alpha Time (A)</strong>: Represents the UTC+1 offset zone (e.g., Central European Time in the winter).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Romeo Time (R)</strong>: Represents the UTC-5 offset zone (which corresponds to Eastern Standard Time in the winter).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Juliett Time (J)</strong>: Represents the observer's local timezone, used to designate local civil time rather than standard military offsets.
      </li>
    </ul>

