  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 12: TECHNOLOGY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="military-time-tech" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Military Time in Technology">
    <h2>12. Military Time in Technology &amp; Databases</h2>
    <p>
      Computers and network infrastructure store logs, database transactions, and network schedules in 24-hour formats. Standardizing to a 24-hour clock prevents errors when scheduling network jobs:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Unix Timestamp</strong>: Under the hood, modern operating systems like Linux and Windows calculate time as the number of seconds that have elapsed since January 1, 1970 (known as the Unix Epoch). This integer is completely independent of local time zones and is formatted into a 24-hour string when displayed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>ISO 8601 Standard</strong>: The International Organization for Standardization established **ISO 8601** to standardize dates and times. It uses a 24-hour format: `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`. The 'Z' suffix designates UTC (Zulu) time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Database Logs</strong>: Database systems like PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MongoDB store timestamps in UTC. When generating reports or sorting query logs, the 24-hour format prevents errors during daylight saving time shifts (e.g., when the 1:00 AM hour occurs twice in November).
      </li>
    </ul>

