  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 13: COMMON MISTAKES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="common-mistakes" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Mistakes">
    <h2>13. Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
    <p>
      Review these common pitfalls when learning to read and format the 24-hour military clock:
    </p>

    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Mistake 1: Confusing 2400 and 0000</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> While 2400 and 0000 refer to the same moment, 2400 is used to represent the exact end of a calendar date, whereas 0000 represents the beginning of the next calendar date. For example, if a tactical patrol ends at midnight on July 15, it is logged at 2400 on July 15. If a patrol starts at midnight on July 16, it is logged at 0000 on July 16.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Mistake 2: Reading 1300 as "Thirteen Thousand"</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> The military format uses "hundred" to represent the hour. Therefore, 1300 is read as "thirteen hundred hours," not "thirteen thousand."
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Mistake 3: Omitting Leading Zeros</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> Military time always requires four digits. Writing "800" instead of "0800" is incorrect and can lead to parsing errors in automated systems.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Mistake 4: Inserting Colons in Military Logs</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> Traditional military communication completely omits the colon (e.g., 1930). Using a colon (e.g., 19:30) is the standard civil 24-hour clock format, not military time.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 14: EXPERT TIPS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="expert-tips" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Expert Tips">
    <h2>14. Expert Tips &amp; Memory Tricks</h2>
    <p>
      Use these practical tips to easily read and memorize 24-hour military clock values:
    </p>

    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>The Subtraction Rule for Afternoon Hours</h3>
      <p>
        An easy trick to read military hours after noon is to subtract 12 from the first two digits. For example, if you see **1700**, subtract 12 from 17 to get 5 (5:00 PM). If you see **2200**, subtract 12 from 22 to get 10 (10:00 PM).
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>The Addition Rule for Standard Time to Military Time</h3>
      <p>
        To convert PM hours to military time, add 12 to the hour value. For example, if a flight departs at 6:00 PM, add 12 to 6 to get 18, yielding **1800** in military time.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Spoken Pronunciation Practice</h3>
      <p>
        When practice reading clocks, pronounce the numbers as the military does. For example, read 0930 as "zero nine thirty" and 1400 as "fourteen hundred." This builds mental familiarity with the 24-hour rhythm.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

